  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1210 PM CDT Sun Sep 13 2026\n\nH5 heights running 590-592 DM today will increase to 596-599 DM\nover the next 48-72 hours; that places it on the top of the\nE-SAT charts for anamolous height rise this time of year.\nSimilarly, H8 temps increase 3-4C as a ridging nose of 23C \nworks overtop the FA, coincident with the setting of the anti- \ncyclone's center just to our southeast and not too far off our\nprevious heat wave's H8 temps. Dew points, after a brief \nreprieve today-tmrw (for parts of the region), mostly return to\n70F+ by Tuesday. The net result will be dangerously high heat \nindices in the mid week period, particularly by Tuesday and\nextending thru at least Thursday, with daily highs in the mid- \nupper 90s combining with 70-75F dews for HI's topping out close \nto/around the Advisory threshold of 105 degrees. LREF only has \n10-20% chances of >104F HI's Tuesday sneaking into the MO \nBootheel, but it's been a consistent modeling of synoptic \nfeatures that suggests the realized ambient air temps, dew \npoints, and resultant HI's should in fact approach or potentialy\nreach/exceed 105F daily in most locales, despite the ensemble- \nclustering tool's apparant smoothing. Interestingly enough, even\nthe LREF has a 70-80% chance of HI's into the triple digits, so\nit's close regardless. And those high temps approach records:\n\nRecords vs Fcst Temps...\nPAH Tuesday 102 vs 96, Wednesday 97 vs 96, Thursday 98 vs 95\nEVV Tuesday 99 vs 97, Wednesday 97 vs 97, Thursday 96 vs 96.\n\nAlso note our Ozark counties and potentially nearer the \nBootheel counties see triple digits that flirt with Advisory \nthresholds as early as Monday, suggesting duration impacts at \nplay in some of our south. Then this gets more widespread \nbeginning Tuesday, and lasting at least thru Thursday. The NWS \nHeat Risk Experimental shoots us into Major by Tuesday, where it\nmore or less holds thru Thursday (with some contraction). After\nThursday, the strength of the ridge is impacted, as our heights\nare increasingly muddied down by rimfire from approaching areas\nof low pressure both to the north and south of the region. As a\nresult, while still remaining above the climo normals of \nlower-middle 80s for mid Sept highs, we do cool off enough to \npreclude the headline worthy triple digit indices of the \nprevious days, as we come to the week's conclusion.\n\nRain chances in the near and long term are small and peripheral\nenough so as to regard little mention other than their presence;\nthis is similar for any night-time patchy fog potential.",
